What is vendored here is the SKILL.md only — inert markdown that instructs
the agent to treat a candidate skill as untrusted input, run two independent
review lines (static scan + source-aware semantic review), and return a
verdict of APPROVE / CAUTION / REJECT. It installs no code and runs nothing
on its own.

The scanner it prefers, the `skillspector` CLI, is deliberately NOT vendored
and NOT auto-installed. The skill degrades to a manual source review and says
so explicitly when the CLI is absent. To add the scanner, install it
separately and keep it out of this repo:

    uv tool install skillspector
    # or, no Python required:
    docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/scan" skillspector scan ./my-skill/ --no-llm

Egress, per upstream's own trust model:
  - It never executes the skill being scanned; analysis is regex, Python AST
    and YARA over file contents.
  - LLM semantic analysis is ON by default and uploads eligible file contents
    to the configured provider. The vendored SKILL.md invokes the CLI with
    `--no-llm`, which keeps contents local. Keep that flag.
  - The SC4 supply-chain check queries OSV.dev with declared dependency names
    and versions (not file contents), even under `--no-llm`, and falls back to
    a bundled list when offline.
  - Providers are configurable via SKILLSPECTOR_PROVIDER; OPENAI_BASE_URL
    accepts any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, so the local LM Studio endpoint in
    home/local-llm can serve the LLM stage without leaving the machine.
  - It is a pre-install screen, not a sandbox. It does not contain a skill you
    install anyway.

Changes in this copy: none to SKILL.md. This NOTICE was added for attribution
and to record the egress caveats above.
